export const HE6={title:'Teaching materials and media',hook:'Handouts, slides, videos, games and models bring a lesson to life—and they appear twice in the scoring. The portfolio awards 10 points for exceptional teaching materials, and Round Two awards 10 points for describing how your team included original or appropriate media and software. Good materials are clear, accurate, engaging and made for your audience.',objectives:['Choose materials and media that support objectives.','Design clear, accessible handouts and slides.','Use original or prepared media legally and appropriately.','Document materials for the portfolio.'],sections:[
['Types of materials and media',[
'Teams may **create their own media** or use **prepared media** (video, software or print). Common materials include **slides**, **handouts and worksheets**, **posters**, **models and demonstration props**, **games** (cards, board games, online quizzes), **videos**, **scripts** for role-plays and **take-home cards** for families.',
'Choose materials that **directly support your objectives**. A sugar-cube demonstration teaches comparing drinks; a role-play script teaches refusal skills; a manikin teaches CPR. Avoid materials that are fun but unrelated.',
'Materials can also include photographs of the lesson and multimedia printouts in the portfolio.',
"Mix material types so different learners stay engaged: something to see, something to hear and something to touch or do. A lesson that relies on a single type of material can lose learners who prefer other ways of learning.",
match('Objective and best material',[['Demonstrate handwashing','Glitter “germs” and a sink demonstration'],['Compare added sugar','Sugar cubes beside drink bottles'],['Practice refusal skills','Role-play scenario cards'],['Identify stroke signs','F.A.S.T. poster and quiz game'],['Perform CPR compressions','Manikins and a tempo song'],['Plan healthy sleep','Personal sleep-plan worksheet']])]],
['Designing clear handouts',[
'Handouts should follow **plain language** principles: a clear title, short sentences, common words, active voice, headings and bullet points. Put the **most important message first**. Use **visuals** that explain, not decorate, and leave white space.',
'Use readable fonts (at least 12 point for adults; larger for older adults and young children), strong contrast and consistent formatting. Include a credible source and, where helpful, a resource such as a hotline or website.',
'Test the handout by asking someone from the audience group to explain its main point.',
"For multilingual audiences, ask the host whether translated handouts would help. Many government health agencies publish materials in several languages that you can use or adapt with credit.",
sort('Good handout practice?',['Good practice','Poor practice'],[['Main message at the top',0],['Long paragraphs of small text',1],['Bullet points and headings',0],['Medical jargon without definitions',1],['A resource such as Poison Help',0],['Decorative clip art covering text',1]])]],
['Slides and visual aids',[
'If you use slides, keep them **simple**: one idea per slide, large text, few words and strong images. Slides support the presenters—they should not be a script read aloud. Use animations sparingly.',
'Physical **visual aids** such as models, real products (empty, clean containers) and demonstrations are often more memorable than slides, especially for younger learners.',
'Always have a **backup plan** for technology failure, such as printed visuals.',
"Practice with your slides or props before the lesson so you know exactly when to show each one. Smooth use of materials looks professional and keeps attention on the message instead of the equipment.",
"If you show a video, keep it short—usually under three minutes—and explain before it begins what learners should watch for. Pause afterward to discuss it rather than moving straight on.",
chk('Which slide design is most effective?',['One idea with a large image and a few words','Every word of the script in small font','Ten bullet points per slide','Flashing animations on every line'],'Simple slides support the speaker.')]],
['Games, technology and software',[
'Interactive **software** and games—online quiz tools, polling apps, digital escape rooms or simple apps—can boost engagement. Choose tools that are **age-appropriate**, **accessible** with the available devices and **privacy-safe** (no unnecessary accounts or personal data, especially for minors).',
'Low-tech games—relays, bingo, card sorts, true-or-false movement games—work anywhere and often engage young learners just as well.',
'In Round Two, describe **why** you chose each medium and how it helped learners meet objectives—that is what judges score under inclusion of media and software.',
"Test any software on the actual devices and network you will use. School networks sometimes block websites or apps, so confirm access with the host in advance and keep a paper version ready.",
sort('Good media choice for a fourth-grade class without devices?',['Good choice','Poor choice'],[['Picture card sort game',0],['An app requiring student email accounts',1],['A short captioned video shown on the classroom screen',0],['A text-heavy website students read alone',1],['A movement-based true-or-false game',0]])]],
['Copyright and permissions',[
'Original materials are best for creativity and originality scores. When using **prepared media**, follow copyright law: use materials that are public domain (many U.S. government publications), licensed for educational use or used with permission, and **credit the source**.',
'Show videos from official sources rather than downloading copies without permission. Do not alter others’ materials in ways their license forbids.',
'List all prepared media on your reference page.',
"Original materials also help you explain your creativity in Round Two. Judges score creativity and originality, and describing a game, song or demonstration your team invented shows effort beyond reusing existing resources.",
match('Media and correct use',[['CDC fact sheet','Usually public domain; credit CDC'],['YouTube video from a health agency','Stream from the official channel and cite it'],['Creative Commons image','Follow license terms and attribute'],['Team-made game','Original; describe it as your own'],['Commercial worksheet','Use only with permission or license'],['Song lyrics written by the team','Original material']])]],
['Documenting materials in the portfolio',[
'The **materials** section of the portfolio may include **photographs of the lesson**, **handouts**, **scripts**, **worksheets** and **multimedia printouts** such as slide thumbnails. Choose examples that highlight the quality of your instruction, and label each with a short caption explaining how it was used.',
'Use photos only with permission, avoid showing identifiable minors unless consent policies allow it and crop out unrelated details. Because the portfolio has 20 pages, select the strongest examples rather than including everything.',
"Keep an inventory of all materials used, including quantities. It helps you prepare for future lessons and lets you describe the scope of your materials accurately in the narrative.",
ex('Captioning a material','Write a caption for a photo of a sugar-cube demonstration (details invented for practice).',['Name the material: Sugar-cube drink display','Explain its use: students guessed sugar in each drink','Connect to objective: compare added sugar','Note permission: photo taken with school approval, no faces'],'“Sugar-cube drink display used to help students compare added sugar (Objective 2); photo taken with school approval.” (Details invented for practice.)')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choosing media',{a:{text:'Your team wants to show a popular commercial video downloaded from an unofficial site. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the better choice?',choices:[['Stream an official health-agency video or make your own short demonstration','b','Correct.',true],['Download it anyway','b','Respect copyright and source credibility.',false]]},b:{text:'Your handout is a full page of small text. How do you fix it?',choices:[['Put the main message first, use headings, bullets, a visual and a resource','c','Correct.',true],['Make the font smaller to fit more','c','Plain language and white space help.',false]]},c:{text:'Your materials are clear, legal and aligned with objectives.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Materials and media—slides, handouts, models, games, videos, scripts, take-home cards—must support objectives; teams may create or use prepared media.','Design plain-language handouts (main message first, headings, bullets, visuals, resources) and simple slides; prefer memorable demonstrations; choose age-appropriate, accessible, privacy-safe technology and plan backups.','Follow copyright (public domain, licenses, permission, credit); document strong materials with captions and permissions in the portfolio; in Round Two, explain why each medium was chosen.'],terms:[['Prepared media','Existing videos, software or print materials used in a lesson.'],['Plain language','Writing that the audience can understand the first time they read it.'],['Visual aid','An object or image that helps explain a concept.']],mcqs:[q('Which portfolio item awards points for teaching materials?','Materials','Title page','Max pages','Formatting','10 points.'),q('Which Round Two item scores media?','Inclusion of media/software','Voice','Diction','Team participation','10 points.'),q('Where should a handout’s main message appear?','At the top','At the bottom','Hidden','Nowhere','Plain language.'),q('An effective slide has:','One idea, few words and a strong image','Every word of the script','Twenty bullets','Constant animation','Simple.'),q('Which media choice is privacy-safe for minors?','A tool that requires no personal accounts','An app collecting home addresses','A site requiring social media logins','Recording students without consent','Privacy.'),q('Many U.S. government publications are:','Public domain','Always copyrighted','Illegal to share','Secret','Free use with credit.'),q('Photos in the portfolio should:','Be used with permission and captioned','Show identifiable minors without consent','Be unrelated','Fill all 20 pages','Permission.'),q('In Round Two, teams should explain:','Why they chose each medium and how it helped','The price of each item','Only the colors used','Nothing about media','Rubric.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the materials and media your team would use for a health lesson and how you would design, use legally and document them.',rubric:[['Materials aligned',['handout','slides','model','game','video','objective','demonstration']],['Design',['plain language','main message','bullets','visual','font','contrast']],['Technology and copyright',['age-appropriate','privacy','backup','public domain','permission','credit']],['Documentation',['portfolio','photos','caption','permission','media','explain']]],model:'For a hand hygiene lesson with second graders, our materials would directly support our objectives. To teach that germs spread by touch, we would use a glitter “germ” demonstration, and to teach the steps of handwashing, a large picture poster and a 20-second handwashing song we wrote ourselves. A team-made “germ detective” card game would let students sort clean and unclean actions, and a take-home handout for families would list the main message at the top—wash hands with soap for 20 seconds before eating and after the bathroom—with simple pictures, headings, large font and a CDC source. We would use only a few simple slides with one large image each and bring printed versions in case the projector failed. We would avoid apps that need student accounts to protect privacy. For a short video, we would stream an official CDC handwashing video and credit it, since many government materials are public domain, and our song, game and handout would be original. In the portfolio, the materials section would include photos of the demonstration taken with school permission and no student faces, the handout, the game cards and slide thumbnails, each captioned with how it was used and which objective it supported. In Round Two, we would explain why each medium fit second graders and how it helped them meet our objectives.'},sources:[RULES,PLAIN,COPY]};

export const HE7={title:'Teaching the lesson to a real audience',hook:'Teaching day is where your planning meets real learners. Students may be more energetic, quieter or more curious than you expected; technology may fail; a question may surprise you. Delivering a lesson well—and collecting evidence of it—is what the portfolio and Round Two presentation are built on.',objectives:['Prepare logistics for teaching day.','Use classroom management and engagement techniques.','Adapt in real time to learners’ responses.','Collect evidence of the lesson professionally.'],sections:[
['Before teaching day',[
'Confirm the **date, time, room, number of participants** and **technology** with the host a few days before. Prepare and count materials, charge devices, print backups and pack a supply kit. Review the timed outline and each member’s role.',
'Arrive early to set up the room, test technology and greet the host. Dress professionally—your appearance sets the tone and models respect for the audience.',
'Bring your evaluation tools (pre- and post-tests or surveys) and a plan for collecting them.',
"Plan who will do what during setup. One member can arrange the room, another can test technology and a third can lay out materials, so the team is ready and calm when learners arrive.",
order('Teaching-day preparation',['Confirm details with the host','Prepare and count materials','Print backups and pack a kit','Review roles and timing','Arrive early and set up','Test technology and greet the host'])]],
['Managing the room',[
'Set **expectations** at the start in a friendly way: “When the hand goes up, voices go down.” Use names when possible, move around the room and give clear, step-by-step directions for activities. Assign team members to support small groups.',
'Keep energy positive. Praise effort, redirect off-task behavior calmly and quietly and keep activities moving. For younger audiences, use attention signals, short segments and movement.',
'The host teacher is a partner; follow classroom rules and ask for help if needed.',
"Positioning matters. Spread team members around the room during activities so every group has support, and stand where all learners can see you during demonstrations.",
sort('Effective classroom management?',['Effective','Ineffective'],[['Using a clear attention signal',0],['Shouting over students',1],['Giving step-by-step directions',0],['Ignoring off-task groups',1],['Praising participation',0],['Embarrassing a student publicly',1]])]],
['Engaging learners',[
'Ask **questions** throughout—open-ended (“What would you do?”) and quick checks (“Thumbs up if…”). Call on a variety of learners and give **wait time** of a few seconds before answering for them.',
'Connect content to learners’ lives: “Which of these drinks do you see at lunch?” Use enthusiasm, movement, humor that respects everyone and real objects. Encourage learners to share ideas and experiences without requiring personal disclosures on sensitive topics.',
'Engaged learners give more meaningful feedback at the end.',
"Encourage participation from quieter learners with low-pressure options such as pair discussions, writing an answer on a card or voting with thumbs. Not everyone wants to speak in front of the whole group.",
"Show genuine interest in learners’ answers by responding to what they say rather than moving straight to the next question. A short follow-up such as “Why do you think that?” encourages deeper thinking.",
chk('What is wait time?',['Pausing a few seconds after asking a question so learners can think','Waiting for the host to leave','Ending the lesson early','Skipping questions'],'More learners respond.')]],
['Adapting in real time',[
'Watch for **signs of confusion** (blank looks, wrong answers) or **boredom** (restlessness, side talk). Respond by re-explaining with a new example, shortening a segment, adding movement or switching to a backup activity.',
'If learners already know a concept from the pre-test, move faster to higher-level activities. If a question goes beyond your knowledge, say so honestly and offer to follow up or point to a reliable resource. If a learner shares something concerning on a sensitive topic, inform the host privately.',
'Note adaptations for your portfolio—they show reflective, responsive teaching.',
"Keep a team member responsible for watching time. A quiet signal, such as holding up fingers for minutes remaining, helps presenters adjust without interrupting the flow of the lesson.",
sort('Good real-time adaptation?',['Good adaptation','Poor response'],[['Giving a new example when learners look confused',0],['Repeating the same words louder',1],['Adding a movement break for restless students',0],['Guessing at an answer to a hard question',1],['Telling the host privately about a concerning comment',0]])]],
['Collecting evidence',[
'Plan how you will document the lesson: **photographs** (with permission, often without identifiable faces of minors), **completed worksheets** or products (with names removed), **pre- and post-tests**, **surveys**, **attendance counts** and **notes** on what happened. The title page asks for the number of participants.',
'Assign one member to take photos and another to collect evaluations. A **host feedback form** or a short note from the teacher can add credible evidence of the lesson’s value.',
'Store evidence safely and protect privacy.',
"Write brief field notes during or right after the lesson: what learners said, which activities sparked the most energy and what surprised you. These details make the narrative and presentation vivid and believable.",
match('Evidence and what it shows',[['Photos of activities','The lesson happened and was engaging'],['Pre- and post-tests','Change in knowledge'],['Learner survey','Satisfaction and confidence'],['Completed swap cards','Learners created action plans'],['Host feedback form','Value from the host’s perspective'],['Attendance count','Number of participants']])]],
['After the lesson',[
'Thank the learners and host. Within a day or two, send a written **thank-you note** to the host. As a team, hold a **debrief**: What went well? What would we change? Which objectives were met, and how do we know?',
'Record reflections while they are fresh; they will strengthen your narrative and Round Two presentation. If results show an objective was not met, consider revising and teaching again if time allows.',
"If the host invites you back, consider teaching a revised version to another group. A second lesson, informed by your evaluation results, makes a strong accomplishment to share with judges.",
ex('Team debrief notes','Summarize a debrief (details invented for practice).',['What went well: the sugar-cube hook captured attention','Challenge: the scavenger hunt ran 4 minutes long','Evidence: 21 of 24 students identified added sugar on the post-quiz','Change next time: smaller groups for the hunt','Follow-up: thank-you note to the teacher'],'Reflection connects evidence to improvements (details invented for practice).')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Teaching day',{a:{text:'Halfway through your lesson, several students are restless and talking. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Use the attention signal and switch to the movement game earlier','b','Correct.',true],['Keep lecturing louder','b','Adapt to the learners.',false]]},b:{text:'A student asks a detailed question you cannot answer. What do you say?',choices:[['“Great question—I’m not sure, but I’ll check a reliable source and let your teacher know.”','c','Correct.',true],['Make up an answer','c','Accuracy matters.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish on time and collect post-quizzes from every student.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Confirm logistics with the host, prepare materials and backups, review roles, dress professionally and arrive early to set up and test technology.','Manage the room with clear expectations, attention signals and step-by-step directions; engage with questions and wait time; adapt in real time with new examples, movement or backup activities; answer honestly when unsure.','Collect evidence—photos with permission, pre/post-tests, surveys, products, host feedback and participant counts—protect privacy, thank the host and debrief to connect evidence to improvements.'],terms:[['Wait time','Pausing after a question to give learners time to think.'],['Debrief','A team discussion reflecting on what happened and what to change.'],['Attention signal','A cue that tells learners to stop and listen.']],mcqs:[q('What should you confirm with the host before teaching?','Date, time, room, participants and technology','The judges’ names','Your final score','Nothing','Logistics.'),q('Which is effective classroom management?','A clear attention signal','Shouting','Ignoring off-task groups','Embarrassing students','Respectful.'),q('Wait time helps:','More learners think and respond','End the lesson faster','Avoid questions','Skip activities','Thinking time.'),q('If learners look confused, you should:','Re-explain with a new example','Repeat the same words louder','Ignore it','End the lesson','Adapt.'),q('If you cannot answer a question, you should:','Say so honestly and follow up','Make something up','Ignore the student','Change the subject angrily','Accuracy.'),q('Which shows change in knowledge?','Pre- and post-tests','Photos only','A thank-you note','Attendance only','Evaluation.'),q('Photos of minors should:','Follow consent policies, often without identifiable faces','Show full names','Be posted publicly','Be taken secretly','Privacy.'),q('What is a debrief?','A team reflection on the lesson','A test for learners','A type of handout','A judge’s score','Reflection.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would prepare for, deliver and document a health lesson for a real audience.',rubric:[['Preparation',['confirm','host','materials','backup','roles','arrive early','technology']],['Delivery',['attention signal','directions','questions','wait time','engage','praise']],['Adaptation',['adapt','confused','example','movement','honest','follow up']],['Evidence and reflection',['photos','permission','pre','post','survey','participants','thank-you','debrief']]],model:'A few days before teaching a 30-minute lesson on lower-sugar drinks to 24 fifth graders, we would confirm the date, time, room, number of students and projector access with the teacher, count and pack our sugar cubes, label cards and drink cartons, print backup visuals and post-quizzes and review each member’s segment and timing. On teaching day, we would arrive early in professional dress, set up stations and test the projector. We would begin by setting a friendly attention signal and giving clear, step-by-step directions, with team members supporting small groups during the scavenger hunt. We would ask open questions such as “Which drinks do you see at lunch?” and use wait time so more students respond. If students looked confused about serving sizes, we would give a new example with a real bottle; if they grew restless, we would start the movement game early; and if someone asked a question we could not answer, we would say so honestly and follow up with the teacher. To document the lesson, one member would take photos of activities with school permission and no identifiable faces, another would collect pre- and post-quizzes and drink-swap cards with names removed, and we would ask the teacher to complete a short feedback form and record the number of participants. Afterward, we would send a thank-you note and hold a team debrief on what worked, what to change and which objectives our evidence showed we met.'},sources:[RULES,PLAIN]};

export const HE8={title:'Evaluating learning and impact',hook:'How do you know your lesson worked? Judges score your feedback tools at 10 points in the portfolio—looking for exceptional examples that collected feedback from audience members—and in Round Two your team must explain the techniques you used to evaluate the effectiveness and impact of your instruction. Evaluation turns a good lesson into a proven one.',objectives:['Distinguish knowledge, skill, attitude and satisfaction measures.','Design pre- and post-tests and surveys aligned with objectives.','Analyze and present evaluation results.','Use results to describe accomplishments and improvements.'],sections:[
['Why evaluate',[
'Evaluation answers two questions: **Did learners meet the objectives?** and **How can the lesson improve?** It provides evidence of your accomplishments for judges and helps you grow as educators.',
'The **CDC Framework for Program Evaluation** describes steps that also apply to a single lesson: engage stakeholders (your host), describe the program, focus the evaluation design, gather credible evidence, justify conclusions and ensure use of findings.',
'Plan evaluation **before** teaching, not afterward—tools must align with objectives from the start.',
order('Evaluation steps (adapted from the CDC framework)',['Engage stakeholders such as the host','Describe the lesson and objectives','Choose evaluation methods','Gather credible evidence','Analyze and justify conclusions','Use and share findings'])]],
['What to measure',[
'Different tools measure different outcomes. **Knowledge** can be measured with quizzes or tests. **Skills** can be measured by observing a demonstration with a **checklist**. **Attitudes and confidence** can be measured with rating scales (“How confident are you that you can…?”). **Intentions** can be measured by asking what learners plan to do. **Satisfaction** can be measured by asking what learners liked and found useful.',
'A 30-minute lesson rarely changes long-term behavior, so focus on knowledge, skills, confidence and intentions—and be honest about what you can and cannot measure.',
'Match each measure to an objective.',
"For larger programs, evaluators sometimes contact learners weeks later to see whether behavior changed. If your host allows it, a brief follow-up survey a week or two after the lesson can provide stronger evidence of impact.",
match('Outcome and tool',[['Knowledge','Pre- and post-quiz'],['Skill','Observation checklist'],['Confidence','Rating scale survey'],['Intention','“I plan to…” question'],['Satisfaction','Feedback survey'],['Host perspective','Teacher feedback form']])]],
['Pre- and post-tests',[
'A **pre-test** before the lesson and an identical or parallel **post-test** afterward show **change** in knowledge. Keep tests short (about five items for a short lesson), aligned with objectives and age-appropriate—picture-based for young children, multiple choice or true/false for older learners.',
'Write clear items with one correct answer, avoid trick questions and keep tests **anonymous** unless the host requires otherwise. You can match pre- and post-tests with simple codes rather than names.',
'Include examples of the tests in the portfolio’s feedback tools section.',
"Give the post-test right at the end of the lesson so learners complete it before leaving. If the host allows it, a short delayed post-test a week later shows whether learning lasted.",
sort('Good test item?',['Good item','Poor item'],[['“Which drink has the least added sugar?” with pictures',0],['A trick question with two correct answers',1],['“True or false: wash hands for 20 seconds.”',0],['A question on content not taught',1],['A short, clear multiple-choice item',0]])]],
['Surveys and observation',[
'**Surveys** use rating scales (for example, 1 = not at all confident to 5 = very confident), yes/no items and open questions (“What is one thing you learned?”). For young children, use smiley-face scales. Keep surveys brief and anonymous.',
'**Observation checklists** list the steps of a skill—such as the six handwashing steps or CPR compressions at the right depth and rate—and team members check each step as learners practice.',
'Open-ended responses can provide powerful quotes for the portfolio and presentation.',
ex('A confidence survey item','Write a confidence item for a refusal-skills lesson (scale only; results come later).',['Stem: “How confident are you that you could say no if a friend offered you a vape?”','Scale: 1 (not at all) to 5 (very confident)','Give before and after the lesson','Compare average ratings'],'A paired rating item measures change in confidence (affective objective).')]],
['Analyzing results',[
'Organize data simply. For tests, calculate the **average score** or the **percentage of learners** answering each item correctly before and after. For surveys, calculate averages or percentages for each rating. Summarize open responses into themes.',
'Present results in a clear **table or bar chart** with labels and the number of participants. Be honest: if results improved only slightly or an item did not improve, explain possible reasons and what you would change.',
"Keep the raw evaluation sheets until after competition in case judges or advisors ask about your data. Record how many learners completed each tool, since not everyone may finish both the pre- and post-test.",
ex('Calculating change','24 students took a 5-item quiz (results invented for practice). The pre-test average was 2.5 and the post-test average was 4.3. What is the change?',['Change = post − pre = 4.3 − 2.5 = 1.8 points','Percent of possible: 2.5/5 = 50% and 4.3/5 = 86%','Improvement: 36 percentage points','Note the item with the smallest gain to revise'],'Average scores rose 1.8 points, from 50% to 86% correct (results invented for practice).')]],
['Describing accomplishments',[
'Use evaluation results to describe **accomplishments** in your narrative and Round Two: “After the lesson, 21 of 24 students correctly identified added sugar on a label, up from 9 before the lesson.” Combine numbers with quotes and host feedback to show impact.',
'Also describe **improvements** you would make based on the data. Judges value reflection and honesty more than perfect results.',
'Round Two specifically asks for techniques used to evaluate effectiveness and impact—describe your tools and your findings.',
"Judges also notice whether your evaluation tools were designed carefully. Explain why you chose each tool and how its items matched your objectives, not just what the scores were.",
chk('Which statement best describes an accomplishment?',['“21 of 24 students identified added sugar on the post-quiz, up from 9 on the pre-quiz.”','“The lesson was great.”','“Everyone loved it, probably.”','“We did a lesson.”'],'Specific evidence.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Evaluation design',{a:{text:'Your only evaluation plan is to ask, “Did you like the lesson?” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is missing?',choices:[['Measures aligned with objectives, such as a pre/post-quiz and a skills checklist','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing','b','Satisfaction alone does not show learning.',false]]},b:{text:'One quiz item did not improve after the lesson. What do you do?',choices:[['Report it honestly and explain how you would revise that part','c','Correct.',true],['Leave it out of the portfolio','c','Honest reflection is valued.',false]]},c:{text:'Your evaluation shows clear accomplishments and thoughtful improvements.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Plan evaluation before teaching, aligned with objectives; the CDC framework guides stakeholders, methods, evidence, conclusions and use of findings.','Measure knowledge (pre/post-tests), skills (observation checklists), confidence and intentions (rating scales), satisfaction (surveys) and host perspective; keep tools short, clear, age-appropriate and anonymous.','Analyze averages and percentages, present results in labeled tables or charts, describe accomplishments with specific evidence and quotes and reflect honestly on improvements.'],terms:[['Pre-test','An assessment given before instruction to measure starting knowledge.'],['Observation checklist','A list of skill steps checked while learners practice.'],['Rating scale','A survey item asking learners to rate on a numbered scale.']],mcqs:[q('Which tool best measures a skill?','Observation checklist','Satisfaction survey','Attendance list','Thank-you note','Observation.'),q('Pre- and post-tests show:','Change in knowledge','Only satisfaction','Room size','Team roles','Comparison.'),q('A confidence rating scale measures which domain?','Affective','Psychomotor','Cognitive only','None','Attitudes.'),q('When should evaluation be planned?','Before teaching','After the competition','Never','During judging','Alignment.'),q('Which is a good test item?','A clear question with one correct answer','A trick question','Content not taught','Two correct answers','Clarity.'),q('If the pre-test average was 2 of 5 and the post-test was 4 of 5, the percentage correct rose from:','40% to 80%','20% to 40%','50% to 60%','10% to 90%','Calculation.'),q('Which statement describes an accomplishment best?','Specific numbers before and after the lesson','“It was great.”','“We tried.”','“Probably good.”','Evidence.'),q('If an item did not improve, you should:','Report it and explain revisions','Hide it','Change the data','Ignore it','Honesty.')],frq:{prompt:'Design an evaluation plan for a health lesson and explain how you would analyze and present the results.',rubric:[['Planning',['before','objectives','aligned','cdc','framework','host']],['Tools',['pre-test','post-test','checklist','survey','rating scale','confidence','anonymous']],['Analysis',['average','percentage','change','table','chart','themes']],['Accomplishments and reflection',['accomplishments','evidence','quotes','improve','honest','revise']]],model:'For a 30-minute hands-only CPR lesson with 28 ninth graders, we would plan evaluation before teaching, aligned with our three objectives, and share the plan with the health teacher. To measure knowledge, we would give an anonymous five-item multiple-choice pre-test and an identical post-test covering when to call 911, where to place hands and the correct compression rate, matched with simple codes rather than names. To measure skill, team members would use an observation checklist as each student practiced on a manikin, checking hand position, depth, rate and minimal pauses. To measure confidence, students would rate before and after, on a 1-to-5 scale, how confident they were that they could perform CPR in an emergency, and an open question would ask what they learned and what they would do if someone collapsed. The teacher would complete a short feedback form. After the lesson, we would calculate the average pre- and post-test scores and the percentage of students answering each item correctly, the percentage of students completing every checklist step and the average confidence ratings, and we would group open responses into themes. We would present results in labeled bar charts with the number of participants. In our narrative and Round Two, we would describe accomplishments with specific evidence, such as the rise in correct answers and confidence, include student and teacher quotes, and honestly explain any item that did not improve and how we would revise that part of the lesson.'},sources:[RULES,EVAL]};

export const HE9={title:'Building the portfolio',hook:'Round One is worth 65 points, and judges see only your portfolio. It must tell the full story of your lesson—goal, research, plan, timed outline, materials and feedback—within 20 pages, in a strict format, uploaded as one PDF. Twenty-five of those points are for format and completeness alone, so careful attention to detail pays off.',objectives:['Include every required portfolio section.','Apply the formatting rules exactly.','Write a strong lesson plan narrative and outline.','Proofread, convert and upload one PDF on time.'],sections:[
['Required sections',[
'The portfolio must include: **(a) Title page**—event name, team member names, HOSA division, chapter number, school name, chartered association, lesson title, target audience age or grade level and number of participants (one page; design does not affect the score). **(b) Lesson plan narrative**—the goal and plan for instruction with data, research and supportive information. **(c) Lesson outline**—a timed outline of the lesson’s segments. **(d) Materials**—photos, handouts, scripts, worksheets and multimedia printouts. **(e) Feedback tools**—tests, evaluations, surveys or other written feedback. **(f) Reference page(s)** in APA style.',
'Pages are counted and numbered beginning with the title page, up to **20 pages** not including references; extra pages are not evaluated.',
"Keep the sections in the order listed so judges can follow the rating sheet easily. Clear section headings, such as “Lesson Plan Narrative” and “Feedback Tools,” help judges find each scored item quickly.",
flip('Portfolio sections',[['Title page','All required identifying details; one page'],['Lesson plan narrative','Goal, plan and research (10 points)'],['Lesson outline','Timed segments; opening to close (10 points)'],['Materials','Examples of teaching materials (10 points)'],['Feedback tools','Evaluation tools and results (10 points)'],['References','APA; not counted in 20 pages']])]],
['Formatting rules',[
'**All pages** must be **one-sided**, **typed**, in **12-point Arial**, **double-spaced**, on **8½ × 11-inch** pages with **1-inch margins**. Every page except the title page needs a **running header** with team members’ last names and the event name at the **top left**, and the **page number** at the top right.',
'The formatting item is all-or-nothing: 5 points if every requirement is met, 0 if any is missed. Set up a template before writing so every section follows it automatically.',
'Captions, tables and handout images should remain readable within these rules.',
"Images, tables and photos of handouts must still fit within the margins. If a handout image becomes too small to read, crop it to the most important part or move a full-size copy to its own page.",
sort('Meets the formatting rules?',['Meets rules','Breaks rules'],[['12-point Arial, double-spaced',0],['11-point Calibri',1],['1-inch margins on letter-size pages',0],['No running header on page 5',1],['Last names and event top left, page number top right',0],['Running header on the title page as well',1]])]],
['Writing the lesson plan narrative',[
'The narrative earns 10 points for an **outstanding description of the goal** and a **thorough outline of the instruction plan**. Include: the topic and why it matters (research and data), the target audience and needs assessment, the goal and measurable objectives, the instructional strategies and materials, team roles, how the lesson was delivered and how it was evaluated.',
'Write clearly and concisely in the third person or as a team (“Our team”), using headings for each part. Cite sources in APA style.',
'A logical narrative helps judges understand every other section.',
"Write the narrative after teaching so you can describe what actually happened, but draft the research and objectives sections earlier while planning. Revising an early draft is much easier than writing everything at the deadline.",
order('A narrative structure',['Topic and research-based need','Target audience and needs assessment','Goal and objectives','Instructional strategies and materials','Team roles and delivery','Evaluation methods and results summary'])]],
['The lesson outline and materials',[
'The **lesson outline** earns full points when it clearly defines key elements with a **timed outline** and a **detailed explanation of execution**, giving clear evidence of a strong opening, delivery and closing. Use a table with times, segments, presenters, activities, materials and linked objectives.',
'The **materials** section showcases exceptional examples—photos of the lesson in action, handouts, scripts, worksheets and slide printouts—each with a brief caption. Choose quality over quantity to stay within 20 pages.',
'Make sure photos are clear and permissions were followed.',
"Include a short explanation with the outline of what actually happened during each segment, such as how learners responded or what you adjusted. This “execution” detail is what separates top-scoring outlines from simple schedules.",
chk('Which earns top points for the lesson outline?',['A timed table with segments, presenters and a detailed explanation of execution','A list of topics with no times','One sentence describing the lesson','Only the title of the lesson'],'Timed and detailed.')]],
['Feedback tools and results',[
'The **feedback tools** section earns full points for **exceptional examples** of tools (tests, written evaluations, surveys) that **provided feedback from audience members**. Include blank copies or samples of each tool, a summary of results (tables or charts) and selected audience comments.',
'Connect results to objectives and accomplishments, and briefly explain improvements you would make.',
"Label each feedback tool clearly—for example, “Pre/Post-Quiz (Objectives 1 and 2)”—so judges can see how it connects to your objectives. A short paragraph after each results chart should explain what the numbers mean.",
ex('Planning page space','Plan a 20-page portfolio (one possible allocation).',['Title page: 1','Narrative: 6','Lesson outline: 3','Materials: 5','Feedback tools and results: 5','Total: 20, plus reference pages'],'A balanced plan covers every scored section within 20 pages.')]],
['Proofreading and submission',[
'Spelling, grammar, punctuation and neatness earn 5 points. Proofread aloud, use spell-check and have an advisor or another reader review the full portfolio. Check that page numbers, headers and fonts are consistent.',
'Export as **one combined PDF**; if it is not a single PDF, judges will not view it and no points are awarded. Links or documents judges cannot access earn no points, so include everything inside the PDF. One member uploads by **May 15 at midnight EST**—no exceptions. Upload early and confirm the file opens correctly.',
'Teams that do not upload are not eligible for Round Two.',
"Name the PDF file clearly, for example with the event and team name, so it is easy to identify. After uploading, download the file from the system if possible to confirm the correct version was submitted.",
order('Final submission steps',['Proofread the full portfolio','Check format, headers and page count','Export one combined PDF','Open and review the PDF','Upload early through the HOSA system','Confirm the upload and save a backup'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Portfolio review',{a:{text:'Your portfolio is 23 pages plus references. (Scenario invented for practice.) What happens?',choices:[['Pages above 20 are not evaluated—trim it to 20','b','Correct.',true],['Judges read everything','b','Excess pages earn no points.',false]]},b:{text:'Your materials section links to a shared drive folder of photos. Is that acceptable?',choices:[['No—include the photos inside the PDF because inaccessible links earn no points','c','Correct.',true],['Yes, judges will click it','c','Include everything in the PDF.',false]]},c:{text:'You upload one complete, correctly formatted PDF well before May 15.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Required sections: title page (all details; one page), lesson plan narrative, timed lesson outline, materials, feedback tools and APA references; up to 20 pages beginning with the title page, references excluded.','Format exactly: one-sided, typed, 12-point Arial, double-spaced, 8½ × 11 with 1-inch margins, running header with last names and event top left and page number top right (except the title page).','Write a thorough narrative, timed outline with execution details, captioned materials and feedback tools with results; proofread; upload one combined PDF by May 15 at midnight EST with no external links.'],terms:[['Running header','Text at the top of each page with last names, event and page number.'],['Lesson plan narrative','The portfolio section describing the goal, research and instruction plan.'],['Feedback tools','Evaluations such as tests and surveys used to measure learning.']],mcqs:[q('What font and size are required?','12-point Arial','11-point Times','14-point Calibri','Any font','Format.'),q('What spacing is required?','Double-spaced','Single-spaced','Triple-spaced','Any spacing','Format.'),q('Where does the page number go?','Top right','Bottom center','Top left','Nowhere','Header.'),q('Which page has no running header?','Title page','Narrative','Materials','Feedback tools','Exception.'),q('What happens to pages beyond 20?','They are not evaluated','They earn bonus points','They are required','They replace references','Limit.'),q('How must the portfolio be uploaded?','As one combined PDF','As several image files','As a website link','On paper','Format.'),q('Inaccessible links in the portfolio earn:','No points','Full points','Bonus points','Half points','Accessibility.'),q('How many points is the portfolio worth?','65','90','155','25','Round One.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would build a Health Education portfolio that earns full points.',rubric:[['Sections',['title page','narrative','outline','materials','feedback tools','references']],['Formatting',['12','arial','double-spaced','1-inch','running header','page number','20 pages']],['Quality content',['goal','research','timed','execution','photos','captions','results']],['Submission',['proofread','one pdf','may 15','links','upload early','backup']]],model:'We would build our portfolio from a template that meets every format rule: one-sided, typed, 12-point Arial, double-spaced, on 8½-by-11-inch pages with 1-inch margins, and a running header with our last names and “Health Education” at the top left and the page number at the top right on every page except the title page. The one-page title page would list the event name, all team members, our division, chapter number, school, chartered association, lesson title, the fifth-grade target audience and 24 participants. In about six pages, the lesson plan narrative would describe the research-based need for a lower-sugar drinks lesson with cited data, our needs assessment, the goal and measurable objectives, our strategies and materials, team roles and how we delivered and evaluated the lesson. Three pages would present a timed lesson outline table with segments, presenters, activities, materials and objectives, plus a detailed explanation of the opening, delivery and close. Five pages would showcase materials—captioned photos taken with permission, our handout, game cards and slide printouts—and five pages would show our pre- and post-quiz, survey and teacher feedback form with result charts and student quotes. APA references would follow and not count toward the 20 pages. We would proofread aloud, have our advisor review it, check headers and page count, export one combined PDF with no external links, open it to confirm it displays correctly and have one member upload it well before May 15 at midnight EST, saving a backup.'},sources:[RULES,APA,UPLOAD]};

export const HE10={title:'The Round Two presentation',hook:'In five minutes—with only notecards and no visual aids—your team must show judges what you taught, why it mattered, how you did it and whether it worked. Round Two is worth 90 points, more than the portfolio, and half of those points judge the quality of the lesson itself. A focused, well-rehearsed presentation brings your project to life.',objectives:['Include all five required presentation elements.','Plan a five-minute presentation shared by every team member.','Highlight objectives, accomplishments and media.','Deliver with strong voice, presence and diction.'],sections:[
['Required content',[
'The presentation **must include**: **(a)** the **goal** of the instructional topic, the **instructional planning process** and **why** you did what you did; **(b)** the **value** of the lesson to the chosen audience; **(c)** the **actual lesson**, highlighting the presentation outline and the use of presentation tools and electronic media; **(d)** the **role each team member played** in preparation and delivery; and **(e)** the **techniques used to evaluate** the effectiveness and impact of instruction.',
'Missing any element weakens several rubric items, so build your plan around all five.',
'The timekeeper announces one minute remaining and stops you at five minutes.',
"Because no visual aids are allowed, your words must create the picture. Practice describing your lesson’s key moments so clearly that judges can imagine the room, the activity and the learners’ reactions.",
match('Required element and example',[['Goal and planning','How we chose the topic and wrote objectives'],['Value to audience','Why fifth graders need to know about added sugar'],['Actual lesson','Our hook, scavenger hunt and drink-swap plan'],['Team roles','Who researched, designed, taught and evaluated'],['Evaluation techniques','Pre/post-quiz, survey and teacher feedback'],['Media','Why we used sugar cubes and a short video']])]],
['How Round Two is scored',[
'**Presentation content** (20 points): objectives and accomplishments of the project (10) and inclusion of media/software (10). **Delivery** (20 points): voice, stage presence, diction and team participation (5 each). **Quality of the lesson** (50 points): health related, meaningful and relevant to the audience, engaging and interesting, appropriate to the targeted audience, and creative and original (10 each).',
'Because judges did not see your lesson, your descriptions must make its quality clear—use vivid examples and evidence.',
'Proper dress for all members earns bonus points.',
"Look at the rating sheet together as a team while planning. For each item, write one sentence of your presentation that will help judges award full points.",
flip('Round Two scoring',[['Objectives and accomplishments','10'],['Inclusion of media/software','10'],['Delivery (four items)','20'],['Quality of the lesson (five items)','50'],['Total','90'],['Bonus','All members properly dressed']])]],
['Structuring five minutes',[
'A clear structure keeps you within time. **Opening (about 30 seconds):** a hook and the lesson topic, audience and goal. **Planning and value (about 1 minute):** how and why you chose the topic, research on need, objectives. **The lesson (about 1½ minutes):** opening, key activities, media and close—described vividly. **Evaluation and accomplishments (about 1 minute):** tools and results. **Roles and close (about 45 seconds):** each member’s contributions and a memorable final line.',
'Teams of up to six should give every member a meaningful speaking part.',
"Mark the one-minute point in your plan. When the timekeeper announces one minute remaining, you should be starting your evaluation results or roles section, not still describing the lesson.",
ex('Timing plan for four members','Plan a five-minute presentation (one possible split).',['Member 1: hook, topic, audience and goal (0:40)','Member 2: research, need and objectives (1:00)','Member 3: the lesson and media (1:30)','Member 4: evaluation and results (1:00)','All: roles and closing line (0:40)','Total: 4:50'],'Every member speaks, and the plan leaves a 10-second margin.')]],
['Showing lesson quality',[
'Judges score the lesson as **meaningful**, **engaging**, **audience-appropriate** and **creative**—so describe it in ways that show these qualities. Explain why the topic was vital for this audience (data and needs assessment), describe the most engaging moments (“Students gasped when we stacked 16 sugar cubes beside one soda”), explain age-appropriate choices and highlight original elements you created.',
'Use short learner quotes and host comments as evidence.',
'Vivid, specific descriptions help judges picture the lesson.',
"Avoid exaggeration. Judges trust specific, honest descriptions, and claims such as “the lesson changed every student’s life” sound less credible than measured results and real quotes.",
sort('Shows lesson quality?',['Shows quality','Weak description'],[['Students gasped when we stacked 16 sugar cubes beside one soda',0],['We did some activities',1],['We wrote an original handwashing song for second graders',0],['It went fine',1],['The teacher said students still talk about drink swaps',0]])]],
['Highlighting objectives and accomplishments',[
'State your **objectives** clearly and connect each to an **accomplishment** backed by evidence: “Our first objective was for students to identify added sugar on a label. On the post-quiz, 21 of 24 students did, up from 9 before.” Mention other accomplishments—such as completed action plans, a request to teach another class or materials shared with families.',
'Explain the **media and software** you used—original or prepared—and why each was effective for this audience.',
'Specific numbers and examples score higher than general claims.',
"Prepare for the possibility that judges remember details from your portfolio. Keep your numbers and descriptions consistent with what you submitted so the presentation and portfolio tell the same story.",
chk('Which best highlights an accomplishment?',['“21 of 24 students identified added sugar on the post-quiz, up from 9.”','“Students learned a lot.”','“We think it worked.”','“The lesson happened.”'],'Evidence-based.')]],
['Delivery and teamwork',[
'Delivery earns 20 points. Speak loudly and clearly, vary rate and volume and pause for emphasis. Use purposeful gestures, good posture, eye contact and enthusiasm. Pronounce terms correctly and avoid fillers such as “um” and “you know.”',
'For **team participation**, the top score requires shared collaboration with **each member speaking and carrying equal parts**. Plan smooth handoffs and support teammates if they lose their place. Use notecards with key phrases, not full scripts; no other materials or visual aids are allowed.',
'Rehearse with a timer and an audience until transitions feel natural.',
"Plan what each member will do while others speak: stand still, look at the speaker or judges and avoid shuffling notecards. Engaged listening contributes to the team’s stage presence.",
order('Rehearsal plan',['Draft each member’s section','Write notecards with key phrases','Practice handoffs','Time full run-throughs','Get feedback on delivery','Polish and rehearse again'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Presentation check',{a:{text:'Your draft presentation does not mention how you evaluated the lesson. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Add your evaluation tools and results—they are a required element','b','Correct.',true],['Skip it to save time','b','Evaluation techniques are required.',false]]},b:{text:'One of your five members has no speaking part. How do you fix it?',choices:[['Rebalance so each member presents an equal part','c','Correct.',true],['Leave it—others speak better','c','Team participation requires equal parts.',false]]},c:{text:'Your presentation covers all five elements, with every member speaking, in under five minutes.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Required elements: goal, planning process and why; value to the audience; the actual lesson with outline, tools and media; each member’s role; and evaluation techniques—within five minutes, using only notecards.','Round Two (90 points): objectives and accomplishments and media/software (10 each), delivery (20) and lesson quality—health related, meaningful, engaging, audience-appropriate and creative (50).','Structure about 4:50 with every member speaking equally; describe the lesson vividly with data, quotes and specific results; deliver with clear voice, presence and diction; rehearse with a timer.'],terms:[['Accomplishment','A specific, evidence-based result of the lesson.'],['Team participation','Every member sharing equal parts of the presentation.'],['Instructional planning process','The steps used to choose, design and prepare a lesson.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is Round Two worth?','90','65','155','50','Presentation.'),q('Which is a required presentation element?','Techniques used to evaluate the lesson','The cost of materials','The judges’ names','A slideshow','Rule.'),q('What visual aids are allowed in Round Two?','None—only notecards','Posters','Slides','Models','Rule.'),q('How many points judge the quality of the lesson?','50','10','20','5','Five items.'),q('Top team participation requires:','Each member speaking and carrying equal parts','One main speaker','The advisor speaking','Only two members speaking','Rubric.'),q('When does the timekeeper signal?','At one minute remaining','At the start','At three minutes','Never','Timing.'),q('Which best describes an engaging lesson?','“Students gasped when we stacked 16 sugar cubes beside one soda.”','“We did activities.”','“It was okay.”','“It happened.”','Vivid.'),q('Electronic notecards are:','Allowed but not shown to judges','Shown on a screen','Prohibited for everyone','Required','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Outline a five-minute Round Two presentation for your Health Education project that includes all required elements and shows lesson quality.',rubric:[['Required elements',['goal','planning','value','lesson','roles','evaluation']],['Lesson quality',['meaningful','engaging','appropriate','creative','original','data','quotes']],['Accomplishments and media',['objectives','accomplishments','post-quiz','results','media','why']],['Structure and delivery',['5 minutes','each member','equal','notecards','handoffs','rehearse','voice']]],model:'Our four-member team would open with a hook: “Sixteen sugar cubes—that is how much sugar our fifth graders discovered in one bottle of soda.” Member 1 would introduce the topic, the 24 fifth-grade students we taught and our goal of helping them choose lower-sugar drinks. Member 2 would explain our planning process: data on children’s sugary drink intake, the teacher’s input and our pre-survey showed a real need, so we wrote three measurable objectives—identify added sugar on a label, compare drinks and create a drink-swap plan. Member 3 would describe the actual lesson following our timed outline: the sugar-cube hook, a label demonstration, a scavenger hunt with real drink cartons, an original drink-swap card and a recap game, explaining why we chose hands-on media rather than many slides for this age group. Member 4 would explain our evaluation techniques—a picture-based pre- and post-quiz, a smiley-face confidence survey and a teacher feedback form—and our accomplishments: 21 of 24 students identified added sugar on the post-quiz, up from 9, and 22 completed drink-swap plans, with the teacher reporting that students still talk about their swaps. Together, we would summarize each member’s role in research, design, teaching and evaluation and close with our slogan. We would use only notecards, give every member an equal part, practice handoffs, rehearse to about 4 minutes 50 seconds and focus on clear voice, eye contact and enthusiasm, all in proper business attire.'},sources:[RULES,EVAL]};
